          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"target_translation\":\"I love you, Your Majesty.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.98,\"reason\":\"This is directly presented as a true statement of Cordelia’s present love for Lear. Its measured qualification concerns the extent and proper basis of that love, rather than changing it into a promise.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":2,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.86,\"reason\":\"The expression explicitly invokes Cordelia’s existing filial bond and is subsequently explained through the duties she returns to her father. It does not create a new promise or undertake unlimited future devotion; Cordelia invokes an already existing daughter-to-father obligation.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Return those duties back as are right fit,\\nObey you, love you, and most honour you.\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.94,\"reason\":\"The expression is a controlled, explicitly calibrated answer, not a reflex-like discharge produced by affect. Cordelia’s difficulty verbalizing her heart does not itself make the eventual assertion an expressive outburst.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Unhappy that I am, I cannot heave\\nMy heart into my mouth: I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":2,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.88,\"reason\":\"Beyond avowing love and invoking filial obligation, the expression functions as a measured refusal to compete in Lear’s demand for maximized declarations. “No more nor less” limits the claim and resists the extravagant comparative terms of the love test.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Which of you shall we say doth love us most?\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"Lear publicly asks his daughters to say which loves him most, linking their answers to the distribution of his bounty. After initially answering “Nothing,” Cordelia gives this restrained avowal, defining her love by her filial bond rather than offering the competitive excess Lear solicits.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"Cordelia invokes an existing filial bond: as Lear’s daughter, she returns fitting duties of obedience, love, and honour. She does not make a new promise or claim that all her love belongs exclusively to him. Lear does not accept the measured answer as satisfactory; he tells her to amend it lest she damage her fortunes.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The supplied text identifies the work as William Shakespeare’s The Tragedy of King Lear, and the scene is recognizable as its opening love test. The classification relies only on the supplied passages.\",\"limitations\":\"The passage strongly supports an avowal and provides an aside supporting Cordelia’s affection, but it does not independently establish every detail of her continuing intentions or later conduct. No omitted context is assumed absent from the work.\"}"
        }
